<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-intro-derivative">
  <title>Tangent Lines and the Derivative</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A line has one slope, and that slope is the same everywhere along it.
      A curved graph is different: it is steeper in some places and shallower in others, and sometimes it is perfectly horizontal.
      So <q>the slope of the graph</q> only makes sense for each particular point.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this activity we measure the slope of a graph at a point by drawing a tangent line there.
      Then we collect those slopes into a brand new function, one that takes an <m>x</m>-value and reports how steep the graph is at that <m>x</m>-value.
      This new function is the central object of the rest of the course.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-derivative-tangent" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: Zooming in on a curve</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Imagine zooming in on a smooth curve at one of its points.
        The closer we look, the straighter the curve appears.
        The line that the curve turns into is the line we want.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Tangent Line</title>
        <p>
          A <term>tangent line</term> to the graph of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a</m> is a line that passes through the point <m>(a, f(a))</m> and that looks like the graph of <m>f(x)</m> when we zoom in close enough to that point.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        The following three views are centered at the same point <m>(1.5, f(1.5))</m> on a graph.
        The solid curve is the graph of <m>f(x)</m>.
        The dashed line is the tangent line at that point.
        Each view is zoomed in more than the one before it.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-derivative-zoom">
        <caption>Zooming in on a graph at <m>(1.5, f(1.5))</m></caption>
        <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%">
          <figure xml:id="fig-derivative-zoom-1">
            <caption>Window width 3</caption>
            <image xml:id="image-derivative-zoom-1">
              <shortdescription>
                A square view of a solid curve and a dashed line crossing at a dot in the center.
                The curve bends noticeably away from the dashed line toward both edges of the view.
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.4cm, y=1.4cm]
                  \def\h{1.5}
                  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
                  \draw[very thick, domain=-1:1, samples=60] plot (\x, {-0.8*\x + \h*(-2*\x*\x/9 + 8*\h*\x*\x*\x/27 + 4*\h*\h*\x*\x*\x*\x/81)/2.5});
                  \draw[thick, dashed] (-1,0.8) -- (1,-0.8);
                  \filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt);
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-derivative-zoom-2">
            <caption>Window width 1.2</caption>
            <image xml:id="image-derivative-zoom-2">
              <shortdescription>
                A square view of a solid curve and a dashed line crossing at a dot in the center.
                The curve stays close to the dashed line and separates only slightly near the edges.
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.4cm, y=1.4cm]
                  \def\h{0.6}
                  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
                  \draw[very thick, domain=-1:1, samples=60] plot (\x, {-0.8*\x + \h*(-2*\x*\x/9 + 8*\h*\x*\x*\x/27 + 4*\h*\h*\x*\x*\x*\x/81)/2.5});
                  \draw[thick, dashed] (-1,0.8) -- (1,-0.8);
                  \filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt);
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-derivative-zoom-3">
            <caption>Window width 0.2</caption>
            <image xml:id="image-derivative-zoom-3">
              <shortdescription>
                A square view of a solid curve and a dashed line crossing at a dot in the center.
                The curve and the dashed line are on top of each other and cannot be told apart.
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.4cm, y=1.4cm]
                  \def\h{0.1}
                  \draw (-1,-1) rectangle (1,1);
                  \draw[very thick, domain=-1:1, samples=60] plot (\x, {-0.8*\x + \h*(-2*\x*\x/9 + 8*\h*\x*\x*\x/27 + 4*\h*\h*\x*\x*\x*\x/81)/2.5});
                  \draw[thick, dashed] (-1,0.8) -- (1,-0.8);
                  \filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt);
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </sidebyside>
      </figure>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-zoom-describe" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe what happens to the solid curve compared to the dashed line as we zoom in.
          Why is the dashed line a good description of the graph near the dot, but only when we are zoomed in?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-zoom-slope" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the views above, is the slope of the tangent line positive, negative, or zero?
          How can you tell without any numbers on the axes?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-derivative-slopes" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: Tangent slopes along a graph</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Here is the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m> shown for <m>-4 \le x \le 4</m>.
        The dots mark the points where <m>x</m> is <m>-4</m>, <m>-3</m>, <m>-1.5</m>, <m>0</m>, <m>1.5</m>, <m>3</m>, and <m>4</m>.
        Each grid square is 1 unit wide and 1 unit tall, with lighter lines halfway between.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-derivative-quartic">
        <caption>The graph of <m>f(x)</m></caption>
        <image xml:id="image-derivative-quartic" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            A smooth W-shaped graph for x from -4 to 4 on a grid.
            It has a local minimum at the point (-3,-4), rises to a local maximum at (0,0), and falls to a second local minimum at (3,-4).
            Dots mark the points at x = -4, -3, -1.5, 0, 1.5, 3, and 4, with y-values about -1.58, -4, -1.75, 0, -1.75, -4, and about -1.58.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm, y=0.9cm, >=stealth]
              \path (-5.8,-5.4) rectangle (5.8,2.4);
              \draw[step=0.5, gray!25, very thin] (-5,-5) grid (5,2);
              \draw[step=1, gray!60, thin] (-5,-5) grid (5,2);
              \draw[->] (-5.3,0) -- (5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->] (0,-5.2) -- (0,2.3) node[above] {$y$};
              \foreach \x in {-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5} {
                \node[below=1pt, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (\x,0) {$\x$};
              }
              \foreach \y in {-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2} {
                \node[left=1pt, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (0,\y) {$\y$};
              }
              \draw[very thick, domain=-4.3:4.3, samples=100] plot (\x, {4*\x*\x*\x*\x/81 - 8*\x*\x/9});
              \foreach \x/\y in {-4/-1.58, -3/-4, -1.5/-1.75, 0/0, 1.5/-1.75, 3/-4, 4/-1.58} {
                \filldraw (\x,\y) circle (2.4pt);
              }
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        We will measure the slope of the tangent line at each dot, record the slopes in a table, and then plot them.
        Estimates are fine.
        Use a straightedge and draw each tangent line so that it matches the graph as closely as possible at its dot.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-derivative-slopes">
        <title>Estimated slopes of tangent lines of <m>f(x)</m></title>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1.5</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
            <cell><m>3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>4</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Slope</cell>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-slopes-horizontal" workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the dots where the tangent line is horizontal.
          Draw those tangent lines on <xref ref="fig-derivative-quartic"/> and record their slopes in the table.
          What is the slope of any horizontal line?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-slopes-steepest" workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Draw the tangent lines at <m>x=-1.5</m> and <m>x=1.5</m> on <xref ref="fig-derivative-quartic"/>.
          These dots are close to the <term>inflection points</term> of <m>f(x)</m>, where the graph switches from bending one way to bending the other and is as steep as it gets between the horizontal tangents.
          Pick two points on each tangent line you drew, use rise over run to estimate its slope, and record the slopes in the table.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-slopes-ends" workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Draw the tangent lines at the two end dots, <m>x=-4</m> and <m>x=4</m>.
          Estimate their slopes and record them in the table.
          How do these slopes compare with the ones at <m>x=\pm 1.5</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-slopes-plot" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Plot the seven points <m>(x, \text{slope})</m> from your table on the axes below.
          Then sketch a smooth curve through them, ending at <m>x=-4</m> and <m>x=4</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-derivative-slope-axes">
          <caption>Axes for the slope of the tangent line</caption>
          <image xml:id="image-derivative-slope-axes" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
              Blank axes on a grid for x from -4 to 4 and for slope values from -6 to 6.
              The horizontal scale matches the graph of f(x).
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm, y=0.45cm, >=stealth]
                \path (-4.8,-6.8) rectangle (4.8,6.8);
                \draw[step=1, gray!60, thin] (-4,-6) grid (4,6);
                \draw[->] (-4.3,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[->] (0,-6.3) -- (0,6.5) node[above] {slope};
                \foreach \x in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
                  \node[below=1pt, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (\x,0) {$\x$};
                }
                \foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {
                  \node[left=1pt, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (0,\y) {$\y$};
                }
              \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-derivative-naming" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: The derivative function</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The curve you just sketched takes each <m>x</m>-value and reports the slope of the tangent line to the graph of <m>f(x)</m> at that <m>x</m>-value.
        That makes it a function in its own right, and it has a name.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>The Derivative</title>
        <p>
          The <term>derivative</term> of <m>f(x)</m> is the function <m>f'(x)</m> (read <q><m>f</m> prime of <m>x</m></q>) whose output at each <m>x=a</m> is the slope of the tangent line to the graph of <m>f(x)</m> at the point <m>(a, f(a))</m>.
          In particular, the graph of <m>f(x)</m> has a horizontal tangent line at <m>x=a</m> exactly when <m>f'(a)=0</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        The input of <m>f'(x)</m> is a position along the graph of <m>f(x)</m>, and its output is a slope.
        Read <m>f'(3)</m> as <q>the slope of the tangent line to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=3</m></q>.
        Keep the two functions apart: <m>f(3)</m> is a height and <m>f'(3)</m> is a slope.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-naming-zeros" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the <m>x</m>-values where your sketch of <m>f'(x)</m> crosses the <m>x</m>-axis.
          Where do those <m>x</m>-values show up on the graph of <m>f(x)</m>?
          Explain the connection.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-naming-estimate" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use your sketch of <m>f'(x)</m> to estimate <m>f'(-2)</m> and <m>f'(3.5)</m>.
          Write what each value tells you about the graph of <m>f(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-derivative-corner" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 4: When a tangent line does not exist</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A tangent line to <m>g(x)</m> at <m>x=a</m> needs two things.
        There must be a point <m>(a, g(a))</m> for the line to pass through, and the graph must look like a line when we zoom in.
        Here we study a graph with a point that is present but where the graph never looks like a line.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-derivative-corner">
        <caption>The graph of <m>g(x)</m></caption>
        <image xml:id="image-derivative-corner" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            A graph made of two parabola arcs that meet at a sharp corner at the point (0,2).
            For x from -3 to 0 the graph is part of a downward-opening parabola with its peak at (-1,3), falling from (-3,-1) up to the peak and back down to (0,2).
            For x from 0 to 4 the graph rises from the corner, reaches a peak at (2,4), and falls to (4,2).
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm, y=0.8cm, >=stealth]
              \path (-3.6,-2.4) rectangle (4.6,5.4);
              \draw[step=0.5, gray!25, very thin] (-3,-2) grid (4,5);
              \draw[step=1, gray!60, thin] (-3,-2) grid (4,5);
              \draw[->] (-3.2,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->] (0,-2.2) -- (0,5.2) node[above] {$y$};
              \foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
                \node[below=1pt, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (\x,0) {$\x$};
              }
              \foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5} {
                \node[left=1pt, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (0,\y) {$\y$};
              }
              \draw[very thick, domain=-3:0, samples=60] plot (\x, {3 - (\x+1)*(\x+1)});
              \draw[very thick, domain=0:4, samples=60] plot (\x, {2 + 2*\x - \x*\x/2});
              \filldraw (0,2) circle (2.4pt);
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The graph has a sharp <term>corner</term> at <m>x=0</m>.
        No matter how far we zoom in on a corner, it still looks like a corner and never like a single line.
        The pieces on either side do each look like a line, though, so we can still ask about the slope that each side is heading toward.
        We write <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0^-} g'(x)</m> for the slope of the tangent line to the piece of the graph immediately to the left of <m>x=0</m>, and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0^+} g'(x)</m> for the slope of the tangent line to the piece immediately to the right.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-corner-sides" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On <xref ref="fig-derivative-corner"/>, draw the tangent line to the left piece of the graph at the point <m>(0, 2)</m>, and the tangent line to the right piece at the same point.
          Estimate their slopes and complete the statements.
          <md>
            <mrow>\lim_{x \to 0^-} g'(x) \amp = \underline{\qquad\quad}</mrow>
            <mrow>\lim_{x \to 0^+} g'(x) \amp = \underline{\qquad\quad}</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-corner-exists" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is there a single number that can be the slope of the tangent line to <m>g(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>?
          Explain why <m>g'(0)</m> does not exist even though <m>g(0)</m> does.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-derivative-corner-sketch" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch <m>g'(x)</m> for <m>-3 \lt x \lt 4</m> on the axes below.
          Use the horizontal tangent lines to place the zeros of <m>g'(x)</m>.
          Estimate the slope at <m>x=-3</m> and at <m>x=4</m>.
          At <m>x=0</m>, mark the two one-sided values from above with open points.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-derivative-corner-axes">
          <caption>Axes for the slope of the tangent line to <m>g(x)</m></caption>
          <image xml:id="image-derivative-corner-axes" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
              Blank axes on a grid for x from -3 to 4 and for slope values from -4 to 4.
              The horizontal scale matches the graph of g(x).
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm, y=0.5cm, >=stealth]
                \path (-3.6,-4.8) rectangle (4.6,4.8);
                \draw[step=1, gray!60, thin] (-3,-4) grid (4,4);
                \draw[->] (-3.2,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[->] (0,-4.3) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {slope};
                \foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
                  \node[below=1pt, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (\x,0) {$\x$};
                }
                \foreach \y in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
                  \node[left=1pt, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (0,\y) {$\y$};
                }
              \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
